      "text": "Given the functional equation \\( f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\), we want to find \\( f(2) \\).\n\nFirst, let's denote \\( y = \\frac{1}{x} \\). Substituting \\( y \\) into the given equation, we get:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\nNow, we have a system of two equations:\n1. \\( f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\)\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously. Add the two equations together to eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\):\n\\[\n(f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)) + (f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x)) = 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(x) - 2 f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}},\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n- f(x) - f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}},\n\\]\nwhich further simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(x) + 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = - (4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}).\n\\]\nNext, we multiply the original equations by -1 and add them together:\n\\[\n- (f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)) = -4^x,\n\\]\n\\[\n- f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = -4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nAdding these two equations, we get:\n\\[\n- f(x) - 3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = -4^x - 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\nf(x) + 3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}.\n\\]\nNow, we have the system:\n\\[\nf(x) = - (4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}),\n\\]\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x - (4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}).\n\\]\nFinally, substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the first equation to find \\( f(2) \\):\n\\[\nf(2) = - (4^2 + 4^{\\frac{1}{2}}) = - (16 + 2) = -18.\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-18}\\).",
