      "text": "Given the functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\]\n\nLet's first define \\( f(2) \\) and subsequently use the properties of function composition to derive \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times f(2) \\) to break down the given functional equation.\n\n### Step 1: Substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation\n\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2 \\]\n\nSince \\( 4^2 = 16 \\), we have:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16 \\tag{1} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the equation\n\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 4^{1/2} \\]\n\nSince \\( 4^{1/2} = 2 \\), we have:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2 \\tag{2} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Combine Equations (1) and (2) to find \\( f(2) \\)\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16 \\\\\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve the system of equations\n\nFirst, we would solve for \\( f(2) \\) and \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\) to combine and eliminate one of them.\n\nSubtract the second equation from the first:\n\\[ (f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)) - (f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2)) = 16 - 2 \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) + 2 f(2) = 14 \\]\n\\[ 3 f(2) - 3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 14 \\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\\[ f(2) - f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{14}{3} \\]\n\nAdd the original equations:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 16 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -f(2) - f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 18 \\]\n\\[ - (f(3) - f(2)) = 18 \\]\n\nFrom these steps we get:\n\\[ f(2) - f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{14}{3} \\]\n\\[ f(3) - 3 f(2) = 6 \\]\n\n### Step 5: Continue with \\( f(4) = 4 f(2) \\)\n\nUse this pattern to generate more terms and solve for \\( f(2) \\).\n\nFinally, by iterating the pattern:\n\\[ f(2) = \\frac{14}{7} = 2 \\]\n\nThus the function value at any point \\( x \\):\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]",
