      "text": "Let's first find a way to solve this problem. We are given that $f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x$. Let's substitute a value of $x$ for which $f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}$ (i.e., when $f(x) = 2$). Solving for $x$, we get $4x = 2$, so $x = \\frac{1}{2}$. Therefore, $f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nNow, let's find the value of $f(2)$ by solving the given equation again. We have:\n$f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2$.\n$f(2) - 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16$.\n$f(2) - 1 = 16$.\n$f(2) = 17$.\n\nThus, $f(2) = \\boxed{17}$.",
